course: world
lesson: 123
title: Pendulums, Greenwich and railway time, atomic clocks and phones
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A good clock needs something that repeats at a steady beat and something that counts the beats. Galileo noticed in a cathedral that a swinging lamp took the same time for each swing, big or small, timing it with his pulse. In 1656 Christiaan Huygens built a pendulum clock, and good clocks went from about 15 minutes wrong a day to about 15 seconds: 60 times better.
explain: A clock can even tell you where you are. The Earth turns 15 degrees an hour, so if your clock still shows London time and the Sun is highest at 2 pm, you are 30 degrees west of London. Pendulums can't keep time on rolling seas, so this was impossible until John Harrison, a carpenter born in Yorkshire, built sea clocks in the 1700s.
explain: Once, every town kept its own Sun time: Bristol's Corn Exchange clock still has two minute hands, 10 minutes apart, for Bristol and London time. Trains needed one time, so British railways used London time from the 1840s, and in 1884 countries chose Greenwich as 0 degrees longitude, the Prime Meridian, from which time zones are counted.
explain: Today a quartz crystal in a watch wiggles 32,768 times a second, and atomic clocks count the "ticks" of caesium atoms so exactly they'd lose less than a second in millions of years. Your phone sets itself from the network or GPS satellites, which carry atomic clocks.
explain: Before that, people set their clocks by shared signals: the red time ball at Greenwich drops at 1 pm every day, the BBC "pips" mark the hour, and in Britain you could dial 123 for the speaking clock.
fact: Galileo noticed that a pendulum takes the same time to swing whether the swing is big or small.
fact: In 1656 Christiaan Huygens built a pendulum clock, far more accurate than any clock before.
fact: A pendulum about 1 metre long takes about 1 second to swing one way.
fact: Good clocks before pendulums could be about 15 minutes wrong in a day, and pendulum clocks about 15 seconds: 900 seconds ÷ 15 = 60 times better.
fact: John Harrison, a carpenter born in Yorkshire, built sea clocks in the 1700s that kept good time on rolling ships, so sailors could find how far east or west they were.
fact: The Earth turns 15 degrees an hour, so each hour of difference between home time and local Sun time means 15 degrees east or west.
fact: The red time ball at the Royal Observatory, Greenwich, has dropped at 1 pm every day since 1833 so ships could set their clocks.
fact: Bristol is about 10 minutes behind London by the Sun; its Corn Exchange clock has two minute hands to show both times.
fact: British railways chose London time, called railway time, in the 1840s.
fact: In 1884 countries chose Greenwich as 0 degrees longitude (longitude says how far east or west a place is), the Prime Meridian.
fact: A quartz crystal in a watch vibrates 32,768 times every second.
fact: The first accurate atomic clock was built in London in 1955; today a second is measured by counting over 9 billion tiny ticks of caesium atoms.
fact: Phones get the time from the phone network or from GPS satellites, which carry atomic clocks.
fact: Britain's speaking clock (dial 123) began in 1936, and the BBC's six "pips" have marked the hour since 1924; the last pip marks the exact start of the hour.
exercise: What did Galileo notice about a swinging lamp? | Each swing took the same time, big or small | Big swings were much faster than small ones | It stopped swinging every minute | It swung faster in the evening
exercise: What did Galileo use to time the swings? | His pulse | A wristwatch | An hourglass | A phone
exercise: Who built the first pendulum clock, in 1656? | Christiaan Huygens | Galileo | John Harrison | Su Song
exercise: About how long does a 1-metre pendulum take to swing one way? | About 1 second | About 1 minute | About 10 seconds | About 1 hour
exercise: How many times better is a 15-second error than a 15-minute error? | 60 | 15 | 900 | 4
exercise: Why couldn't sailors long ago tell how far east or west they were? | They had no clock that kept good time on a rolling ship | They could never see the stars at sea, because of all the clouds | Compasses did not work over water | Nobody had drawn maps of the sea
exercise: Who built sea clocks that kept time on rolling ships? | John Harrison, a carpenter | Galileo, an Italian scientist | Pope Gregory XIII | William Willett, a builder
exercise: The Sun is highest above your ship and your London clock says 2 pm. How far west of London are you? | 30 degrees | 2 degrees | 15 degrees | 60 degrees
exercise: What happens every day at 1 pm at Greenwich? | A red time ball drops down a pole | A cannon fires 13 times over the river | All the clocks there stop for one minute | A big bell rings exactly 1,000 times
exercise: Why does Bristol's Corn Exchange clock have two minute hands? | One shows Bristol Sun time, the other London time | One is for summer and one is for winter | One is a spare, in case the other one ever breaks down | One shows minutes and one shows seconds
exercise: What was "railway time"? | One shared time, London time, for trains | A special time of day when trains ran for free | A clock that ran on steam from the engines | The time it took to build a whole railway line
exercise: What was chosen in 1884? | Greenwich as 0 degrees longitude | London as the capital of the world | The first atomic clock | The date of New Year
exercise: How many times a second does a quartz crystal in a watch vibrate? | 32,768 | 60 | 1,000 | 24
exercise: What does an atomic clock count? | The steady ticks of atoms | The swings of a pendulum | Drips of water | Turns of the Earth
exercise: How does a phone know the time without being set? | From the network or GPS satellites that carry atomic clocks | From a tiny sundial hidden in the screen | It guesses from how full the battery is and how long it has been on | From the alarm its owner sets each morning
exercise: What are "the pips"? | Six short beeps on BBC radio marking the hour | The little seeds you find inside an apple or an orange | A ringtone that came on very old mobile phones | The sound a microwave makes when your food is done
test: Leo times a conker on a string: big swing, small swing, same time. His mate: "Nah, big swings take longer, deffo." Who's right? | Leo: Galileo found the swing time stays the same | The mate: big swings always take longer | The mate: small swings take twice as long as big ones do | Neither: a conker can't swing steadily
test: Ava: "15 minutes off a day down to 15 seconds, that's like sixty times better." Check her sum. | Right: 900 seconds ÷ 15 = 60 | Wrong: it's only 15 times better | Wrong: it's 900 times better | Wrong: it's 4 times better
test: About Harrison's sea clock: "A clock that saves ships? Mint." ("Mint" means great.) How did a clock save ships? | It let sailors work out how far east or west they were | It warned sailors of storms coming by ringing a loud bell | It steered the ship all by itself | It kept the sailors awake at night
test: Mia's brother at Greenwich at 1 pm: "A ball drops down a pole. Is that it?!" Why did it matter? | Ships on the Thames used it to set their clocks exactly | It told sailors the tide was coming in | It warned that the observatory was closing | It was the signal for everyone in London to stop for lunch
test: Jake, from Birmingham: "Two minute hands on one clock, what's the point, bab?" ("Bab" means love or mate.) Why do they make sense? | Before railways, each town kept its own Sun time | Bristol has two different hours in every day | One hand is for the left eye, one for the right | The clockmaker simply made a mistake
test: Lily: "So Greenwich is, like, the boss of time?" Best answer? | Greenwich is 0 degrees longitude, where time zones are counted from | Greenwich decides what time everyone gets up | Greenwich is where the first clock was ever made | Greenwich has its own private time that nobody else in the world uses
test: Arjun: "A crystal in my watch wiggles? Dad's taking the mick." ("Taking the mick" means joking.) Is Dad joking? | No, a quartz crystal vibrates 32,768 times a second | Yes, watches only ever use springs and little wheels | Yes, crystals are a kind of rock and can never move at all | No, but it only wiggles very slowly, about once a minute
test: Ollie: "Atomic clock? So it's never late for school, then." About how good are the best ones? | They'd lose less than a second in millions of years | They lose about a second every single day of the year | They lose about 15 seconds in a single day of ticking | They lose about one whole hour by the end of every year
test: Noah: "My phone knows the time and I never set it, lowkey creepy." ("Lowkey" means a bit.) How does it know? | It gets the time from the network or GPS satellites with atomic clocks | It reads where the Sun is in the sky by peeking through its camera lens all day | It counts how many times you unlock it | It copies the time from the nearest phone
test: Nan: "We dialled 123 for the speaking clock, love." Mila: "The what?" What was it? | A phone number where a voice said the exact time | A clock that answered questions out loud | A talking alarm clock sold in shops | A radio show about old clocks
test: What would happen if you took an ordinary pendulum clock onto a rolling ship? | The rocking would upset the swing and it would keep bad time | It would keep even better time, thanks to the rocking of the waves | It would work exactly as it does on land, with no change at all | It would turn into a proper sea clock all by itself after a while
test: Grandad: "The pips! Six beeps, set your watch on the first one." Fix his mistake. | The last pip marks the exact start of the hour | The first pip marks the start of the hour | The third pip marks the start of the hour | The pips have nothing to do with the hour
exam: Identify the property of the pendulum observed by Galileo. | The period of a swing is the same whether the swing be large or small | The period of a swing doubles whenever the swing becomes twice as large | A pendulum ceases to swing after one minute | A pendulum swings more rapidly in the evening
exam: Given that a navigator's clock indicates two o'clock in the afternoon, London time, at the moment of local noon, and that the Earth rotates fifteen degrees per hour, ascertain the position of the ship. | Thirty degrees west of Greenwich | Thirty degrees east of Greenwich | Fifteen degrees west of Greenwich | Two degrees west of Greenwich
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the international conference of 1884. | Greenwich was adopted as the meridian of zero degrees longitude | Paris was adopted as the meridian of zero degrees longitude | Summer time was adopted throughout Europe | The first atomic clock was demonstrated
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means whereby a mobile telephone ascertains the time? | It receives signals from the network or from satellites carrying atomic clocks | It derives the time from the position of the Sun | It relies exclusively on manual setting by its owner | It measures the swings of an internal pendulum
